'''buff'''<sup>1</sup> (buf), ''n.'' and ''a.'' [Early mod. E. ''buffe'', short for ''buffle''<sup>1</sup>, q. v.] '''I.''' ''n.'' '''1†.''' A buffalo.
{{smaller block|''Buffalo'' [It.], a buffle, a ''buffe''. {{right|''Florio.''}}
''Buffle'' [F.], the ''buffe'', buffle, bugle, or wild ox. {{right|''Cotgrave.''}}
There are also wilde beastes bred in those woods, as ''Buffes'', Beares, and blacke Wolues.
{{right|''Hakluyt’s Voyages'', I. 248.}}
They haue also the qualities of a ''Buffe'': for if they see a man clothed in red, they run vpon him immediately to kill him. {{right|''Hakluyt’s Voyages'', I. 116.}}}}
'''2.''' A kind of thick leather, originally and properly made of the skin of the buffalo, but now also of the skins of other animals, as elks, oxen, etc. {{smaller|It is dressed so as to be as flexible as possible, and without a glazed or artificially colored surface. It is used for making belts, pouches, gloves, etc., and in the later middle ages came into use to take the place in a measure of light armor: as, “a suit of ''buff'',” ''Shak.'', C. of E., iv. 2. Also called ''buff-leather''.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>His doublet was of sturdy ''buff'',
And though not sword, yet cudgel-proof.</poem>
{{right|''S. Butler'', Hudibras, I. i. 305.}}}}
'''3.''' A buff-coat (which see).
{{smaller block|<poem>I’ll make a shift to drain it
Ere I part with boots and ''buff''.</poem>
{{right|''Praed'', Sir Nicholas at Marston Moor.}}}}
